<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Clinical program priorities">
  <div><strong>Start with the use case</strong><span>Each clinical question should specify the population, care setting, hydration problem and outcome before a product claim is considered.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Measure feasibility first</strong><span>Sampling, preparation, adherence, tolerance and clinical workflow must be workable before a larger efficacy study is meaningful.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Predefine decision rules</strong><span>Study findings should determine what happens next, including when to refine, stop, repeat or advance a research program.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Program architecture</p>
  <h2>Four clinical research pillars</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Hydration measurement</h3><p>Determine which combinations of fluid-balance, urine, body-mass, intake, symptoms and context measures are feasible and interpretable for the population being studied.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Preparation & tolerance</h3><p>Assess whether the product can be prepared consistently, consumed as intended and tolerated under the actual conditions of use.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Clinical relevance</h3><p>Select outcomes that matter to the care question rather than relying only on laboratory surrogates that may not translate into meaningful benefit.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Implementation</h3><p>Study workflow fit, staff burden, education, access, adherence and the circumstances in which clinician oversight is necessary.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4266947/pexels-photo-4266947.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Healthcare professional and patient reviewing information together on a tablet" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Question before product</p>
    <h2>A clinical program should begin with a patient or system problem</h2>
    <p>Examples of legitimate research questions include whether a hydration assessment can be made more practical, whether an oral hydration protocol is feasible during a defined recovery period, or whether a formulation is acceptable in a population with specific hydration challenges.</p>
    <p>Those questions are narrower and more useful than asking whether a product is simply “good for patients.” They force the protocol to specify who is being studied, why hydration matters, what comparison is appropriate and what outcome would count as meaningful.</p>
    <p>This structure also makes negative findings useful. If preparation burden is high or a measure is too variable, that result can improve the next protocol even when no product benefit is demonstrated.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Evidence ladder</p>
    <h2>Advance only when the previous layer is credible</h2>
    <p><strong>Rationale</strong> asks whether the question is scientifically plausible. <strong>Feasibility</strong> asks whether the protocol and measurements work. <strong>Safety and tolerability</strong> ask whether the intervention can be used as planned. <strong>Efficacy</strong> asks whether it changes a meaningful predefined outcome. <strong>Effectiveness and implementation</strong> ask whether that benefit survives real-world practice.</p>
    <p>Not every question needs every stage, but later-stage clinical language should not outrun the strongest evidence actually available.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Potential outcome domains</p>
  <h2>Choose measurements that match the clinical question</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Hydration & fluid balance</h3><p>Examples may include intake and output, body-mass change, urine concentration measures, thirst, clinical observations or other context-appropriate measures. No single measure is treated as universally definitive.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Use & tolerability</h3><p>Preparation accuracy, consumption, adherence, gastrointestinal symptoms, palatability and reasons for stopping can determine whether an oral hydration approach is practical.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Clinical & system outcomes</h3><p>Where justified, later studies may examine outcomes meaningful to the population or care pathway. Those endpoints should be specified in the protocol rather than chosen after results are known.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Governance</p>
    <h2>Clinical credibility depends on process, not just results</h2>
    <p>Before human-participant research begins, the research relationship should define sponsor and investigator responsibilities, protocol ownership, data custody, conflict-of-interest management, safety reporting, statistical analysis, authorship and publication rights.</p>
    <p>Applicable studies must also satisfy Research Ethics Board and regulatory requirements. Health Canada states that applicable Phase I to III natural health product clinical trials require authorization before commencement, while its guidance treats certain observational and Phase IV work differently.</p>
    <p>The exact classification of a future Best Hydrate study must therefore be determined from its actual protocol. A website label such as “observational” cannot be used to avoid requirements that the study design triggers.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Protocol rule</span>
    <h3>The study design determines the requirements.</h3>
    <p>Regulatory and ethics obligations should be assessed from what investigators actually do, including assignment, sampling and conditions of use.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Decision gates</p>
  <h2>What should happen after each study stage?</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Question</th><th>Possible next decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Methods / feasibility</strong></td><td>Can participants, staff and investigators execute the protocol and obtain interpretable measurements?</td><td>Refine methods, simplify burden, replace weak measures, or advance to a larger pilot.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Pilot</strong></td><td>Are recruitment, preparation, adherence, safety monitoring and outcome collection workable enough for a definitive study?</td><td>Stop, redesign, estimate a larger study, or narrow the target population.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Controlled evaluation</strong></td><td>Does the intervention change the prespecified outcome relative to an appropriate comparator?</td><td>Replicate, investigate subgroups cautiously, or decide the evidence does not support advancement.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Implementation</strong></td><td>Can the intervention be delivered consistently and appropriately in the intended care setting?</td><td>Refine workflow, training, access and eligibility, or reconsider the use case.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/6011604/pexels-photo-6011604.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Doctor reviewing digital clinical information on a tablet" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
